Another route for obtaining functionalized MOFs is by postsynthetic modification (PSM) (Fig. 3).12 PSM, which was originally suggested by Hoskins and Robson13 in 1990 and formally introduced by Wang and Cohen,14 is defined as the chemical modification of a framework after it has been synthesized. Rather than synthesizing a functionalized MOF directly from a functionalized ligand, a MOF can be synthesized and modified in a heterogeneous manner after the formation of the solid lattice. PSM has proven to be a general, practical approach for the functionalization of MOFs.15,16 PSM can be advantageous compared to the prefunctionalization approach due to greater control over the types and number of functional groups that can be incorporated into the framework. Often, by using PSM, both the metal and organic components can be functionalized without affecting the overall stability of the framework. PSM is an excellent tool for preparing topologically identical, but functionally diverse frameworks. For example, IRMOF-3 (Fig. 1), which is constructed from $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}O}$ secondary building units (SBUs) and 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BDC})}}\end{array}$ ) linkers, can be modified with alkyl anhydrides to generate a series of alkyl functionalized materials with identical framework structures.17 Alternatively, MOFs with different structures (and hence different porosities and pore structures) can be modified with identical substituents.16 In this way, two types of complementary, systematic studies can be performed for the development of structure–function relationships based on the MOF topology versus MOF functionality.
The major PSM strategies for functionalizing MOFs include covalent and coordinate covalent modification (Fig. 4). Covalent modification describes the chemical modification of the organic ligand (linker) component of the MOF. In an early report of covalent PSM, Lee and coworkers prepared a coordination polymer from $\operatorname{AgOTf}$ and (2,4,6-tris(4-ethynylbenzonitrile)phenoxy)ethanol and modified the pendant alcohol group with trifluoroacetic anhydride.18 Similarly, Kim and coworkers performed covalent PSM, using a MOF termed D-POST-1, where free pyridyl groups within the MOF were alkylated with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ and $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{5}\mathrm{I})$ .19 In 2007, Wang and Cohen performed the first detailed study of PSM by modifying the amino groups of IRMOF-3 with acetic anhydride to generate a MOF that contained methyl amide substituents, termed IRMOF-3–AM1 $\mathbf{AM}=$ amide).14
In contrast to covalent modification, coordinate covalent modification involves changes in the coordination environment of the SBUs within the framework that do not alter the overall SBU or the framework topology. There are two general approaches for coordinate covalent modification. In the first scenario, coordinating ligands, such as alkylamine or pyridines, can be introduced into the framework to bind to the metal nodes of the SBUs where unsaturated metal sites are present.20–22 In the second common scenario, the organic linker of the MOF may have metal binding groups (e.g., $-\mathrm{OH})$ that do not play a role in the framework structure, and hence can be metallated in a PSM manner. For example, Lin and coworkers prepared a MOF from $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and $(R)\ –6,6^{\prime}$ -dichloro- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dihydroxy- $1,1^{\prime}$ -binaphthyl$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine and obtained a structure with free, uncoordinated hydroxyl groups that could be metallated with $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ .23
Covalent and coordinate covalent modifications have also been used in tandem (Fig. 4). Not all MOFs have unsaturated metal sites at the SBUs or metal binding groups built into the organic linkers. However, by combining covalent and coordinate covalent modification, the organic linker of the framework can be appended with a ligand and subsequently metallated. This approach is advantageous because different combinations of chelators (e.g., –OH, –COOH) and metal ions can be introduced to the framework. Rosseinsky and coworkers executed the first dual covalent and coordinate covalent modification using IRMOF-3 by converting the free amine groups into Schiff base ligands with salicylaldehyde. The resulting chelator was metallated with ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{3}$ and the material was examined as a heterogeneous oxidation catalyst.24 This latter study inspired several subsequent investigations that are described below.
This review is a progress report on PSM, providing an update from the time the first review appeared on this subject in early 2009.12 The materials presented in this review have all been published since the appearance of the first review in this same journal (dating to approximately June 2008). A special emphasis is given to studies that have demonstrated the ability of PSM to introduce a wider array of functionalities when compared with direct solvothermal synthesis (i.e., prefunctionalization) approaches. PSM studies that have led to potential improvements in applications such as gas sorption and catalysis will be discussed. Finally, studies involving PSM to monitor organic reactions, develop materials for biomedicine, and manipulate the physical properties of MOFs will be discussed.
## 2. New methods in PSM
## 2.1 New PSM reactions
Several studies have focused primarily on identifying organic reactions and reagents that can be used in PSM to create more complex substituents within MOFs. Among these new transformations, Burrows et al. demonstrated the first covalent oxidation of sulfur tagged MOFs in a one-step reaction (Fig. 5).25 IRMOF-9 analogs (Fig. 2) with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2^{+}}$ and sulfur functionalized $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate ligands (2-((methylthio)methyl)- $[1,1^{\prime}$ -biphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ dicarboxylic acid, $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bis((methylthio)methyl)-[1,10-biphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid, and 5,7-dihydrodibenzo[c,e]thiepine-3,9-dicarboxylic acid) were synthesized and oxidized using dimethyldioxirane (DMDO) in acetone. The IRMOF sulfide analogs were found to smoothly convert into their sulfone analogs with conversions ranging from $77\%$ to essentially quantitative. In an unrelated singlestep reaction, Yaghi and coworkers carried out ring opening reactions with the amine-bearing IRMOF-3 to generate sulfonate and alkylamine groups (Fig. 6).26 These groups were introduced by treating IRMOF-3 with either 1,3-propanesultone or 2-methylaziridine in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . For the reaction with 1,3-propanesultone, $57\%$ of the amine sites in IRMOF-3 were converted to the sulfonate group, as gauged by elemental analysis. For the reaction with 2-methylaziridine, the analysis was more complex due to possible polymerization reactions; nonetheless, the overall conversion on a per ligand basis was essentially quantitative. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area measurements confirmed the modification of IRMOF-3 in these experiments as evidenced by decreases in the measurable surface area of the modified MOFs $(\sim2400\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for IRMOF-3 versus $\sim1380\ m^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the sulfonate- and $\sim530~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for alkylamine-derivatives).
The groups of Cohen and Farrusseng have explored functional group transformation reactions to create new reactive groups within the MOF lattice. Volkringer and Cohen transformed the free amine groups of MIL-53(Al)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ into reactive iso(thio)cyanates using diphosgene or thiophosgene.27
Attempts to modify IRMOF-3 and $\mathrm{UMCM-l-NH}_{2}$ with phosgene reagents resulted in the destruction of these MOFs due to the formation of HCl as a byproduct. Therefore, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was used for these reactions because of its greater chemical stability (particularly with respect to acid) when compared with zinc-carboxylate MOFs. Indeed, an earlier report by Ahnfeldt et al. had shown that treating MIL-53(Al)– ${\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}$ with formic acid produced a formamidebearing MOF without degradation of the framework.28 The modified MIL-53(Al)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ materials (termed MIL-53(Al)–NCO and MIL-53(Al)–NCS, Fig. 7) were analyzed by ATR-FTIR to confirm the functional group transformation. MIL-53(Al)– ${\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}$ has distinct $\boldsymbol{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ stretches in the IR spectrum around $3400{-}3500~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ , but after modification with either diphosgene or thiophosgene, the $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ stretches diminish and a new stretch corresponding to the $-\mathrm{NCO}/-\mathrm{NCS}$ group emerges. The new MILs were analyzed for ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake. MIL-53(Al)–NCO and MIL-53(Al)–NCS did not show any significant uptake of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , but both MILs showed high capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption. In contrast, the parent material $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed uptake of both ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , but low uptake of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Finally, the MIL-53(Al)–NCO and MIL-52(Al)–NCS materials were shown to react with amines and alcohols to produce (thio)urea and (thio)carbamatebearing MIL materials.
Another example of new functional group transformations was demonstrated with DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{DMOF}=\mathrm{DABCO-MOF};$ DABCO $\mathit{\Pi}=\ 1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane).16,29 The amino groups of DMOF-1– $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were transformed into azides and coupled to alkynes in a one-pot reaction (Fig. 8).30 The amino group of DMOF-1– $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was converted into an azide using tBuONO and $\mathrm{TMSN}_{3}$ . The intermediate, DMOF– ${\bf\cdot N}_{3}$ , was then treated with an excess of phenylacetylene and $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C N)_{4}P F_{6}}$ to generate the triazole modified DMOF, termed DMOF–fun, via ‘Click’ chemistry with $>90\%$ yield. ‘Click’ chemistry typically refers to the 1,3-dipolar (Huisgen) cycloaddition reaction between an azide and an acetylene catalyzed by copper.31 ‘Click’ reactions on MOFs had been previously reported using either acetylene32 or azide bearing MOFs.33 Compelling evidence for the formation of ${\bf D M O F-N}_{3}$ was provided by the very distinct azide stretch in the IR spectrum at $2123\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . This band appears only after transformation of the amino group to the azide and disappears after reaction with the alkyne. In order to show the generality of the one-pot PSM, MIL-68(In)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was also modified to give $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}68(\mathrm{In}){\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{3}$ and MIL-68(In)–fun, but with lower yields $(\sim50\%)$ .
## 2.2 Increasing and controlling functionality via PSM
PSM has proven to be a relatively straightforward approach for modifying MOFs. Recently, the desire to produce an even wider range of functionalized MOFs of greater complexity has brought forth new challenges in the PSM field. Although PSM has now been firmly established as a viable approach to functionalizing MOFs, there still remains the question of how many different functional groups can be incorporated into a
MOF and how the spatial localization (i.e. crystal interior versus surface) can be controlled. As a result, several groups have stepped up to the challenge of designing new strategies for PSM in order to address these issues.
As mentioned previously, PSM may allow for better control over the types and number of functional groups that can be added to a MOF. Yaghi and coworkers have recently demonstrated that ligands with different functional groups can be mixed to form multivariate MOFs (MTV-MOFs); however, this approach is somewhat limited as the degree of incorporation of different ligands is not entirely predictable.34 In a previous study, Wang and Cohen had proposed tandem PSM, which involves two modifications in sequential order as an alternative approach to multifunctional MOFs.35 In one report, IRMOF-3 was modified with acetic anhydride and crotonic anhydride to produce a MOF with both an alkyl (–AM1) and olefin (–AMCrot) substituent. The modifications were carefully controlled by varying the concentration of the reagents and the reaction time. As a result, both substituents could be introduced into a single framework with predictable and reproducible degrees of modification.35
In a subsequent report, Cohen and coworkers tested the maximum number of functional groups that could be added to the IRMOF-3 framework via tandem PSM.15 It was found that IRMOF-3 could be modified with up to four sequential additions of anhydrides and isocyanates containing a mixture of alkyl, unsaturated hydrocarbon, and aromatic substituents (Fig. 9). Two anhydrides, decanoic anhydride (–AM9) and crotonic anhydride (–AMCrot), and three types of isocyanates, propyl isocyanate (–UR3, UR $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ urea), allyl isocyanate (–URAl), and phenyl isocyanate (–URPh), were selected because the amide and urea modified ligands have distinct aromatic resonances that can be readily distinguished by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR (for analysis of the modified MOFs). Two different functionalized MOFs were prepared (–AM9/–URAl/–UR3/ –AMCrot and –AM9/–URAl/–URPh/–AMCrot) and analyzed by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR and LC-UV MS analysis (upon digestion of the material in acid). $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR confirmed the presence of five different organic linkers, corresponding to the unmodified $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-BDC}}$ , two amide-modified linkers, and two urea-modified linkers in these materials. LC-UV MS analysis of a single modified crystal further corroborated the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR results by showing five distinct components corresponding to the unmodified $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-BDC}}$ and the four modified derivatives, thus providing solid evidence that IRMOF-3 was successfully modified with multiple functional groups. An important limitation was noted, as PXRD indicated that the overall modification had to be limited to $\sim50\%$ of the amine sites to avoid loss of crystallinity.
The task of obtaining spatial control in PSM (i.e., surface versus interior modification) of a MOF has also remained a formidable challenge. Although a single MOF can be modified multiple times, the overall spatial distribution of the substituents is generally not known, and in most cases is assumed to be evenly distributed throughout the crystal. Recently, Kitagawa and coworkers have reported the modification of a single monolayer of a MOF by using coordinate covalent modification to exchange the surface ligands with carboxylate-bearing dyes (Fig. 10).36 The surface modification was performed by using $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ paddlewheel DMOF-1 and a topologically related MOF based on 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (NDC). For this study, it is important to note that in DMOF structures the paddlewheel SBUs form 2D sheets (comprised of the metal ion and BDC ligands), while the DABCO ligands coordinate to the axial positions of the SBUs (Fig. 2). This results in a topology where the MOF contains four crystal faces terminated by carboxylate ligands and two faces terminated by DABCO ligands. In the present case, the carboxylate terminated surfaces were targeted for ligand exchange using boron dipyrromethene (BODIPY) containing dyes that contained carboxylic acid groups. The modification was performed by soaking the DMOF-1 crystals in a solution of the BODIPY dye at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for three hours followed by extensive washing with DMF. The crystals were analyzed by confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) in different orientations to locate the BODIPY dyes. The CLSM data showed that the BODIPY dyes were clearly appended only to the carboxylate surfaces and not the amine surfaces of the crystals. No dye was detected within the interior of the MOFs. This elegant study clearly showed that surface modification of MOFs could be achieved through simple ligand exchange.
Hupp and Nguyen et al. developed a clever method to selectively modify both the surface and interior of a MOF.37 By taking advantage of solvent molecules trapped within the framework, the ability to distinguish the exterior and interior of the MOF was achieved. As-synthesized TO-MOF, a $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ paddlewheel structure formed from 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) benzene (TCPB) and 3-[(trimethylsilyl)ethynyl]-4- [2-(4-pyridinyl)ethenyl]pyridine (Fig. 11), was treated with triethylammonium fluoride (TEAF) in a $\mathrm{THF/H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mixture to quantitatively deprotect the trimethylsilyl (TMS) groups and produce terminal alkynes throughout the lattice. In order to confirm the deprotection, the MOF was analyzed by IR, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption analysis, and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR digestion. The IR stretch of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}\equiv{\mathrm{C}}$ triple bond was found to shift from 2155 to $2105\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ upon deprotection, thus indicating that the TMS-protected alkyne was converted to the terminal alkyne. The terminal alkyne groups in TO-MOF were treated with benzyl azide to form a triazole via the ‘Click’ reaction. After coupling, the $\mathrm{{{C}\equiv\mathrm{{C}}}}$ triple bond stretch disappeared from the IR spectrum and the surface area of the triazole-appended MOF was significantly lower than both the deprotected and protected TO-MOF (as expected based on the greater bulk of the triazole group).
In order to perform a selective deprotection on this system, TO-MOF was solvent exchanged with $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ (to remove DMF from the pores), followed by exposure to a solution of potassium fluoride (KF) in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Under these conditions, KF can only access the surface of the MOF because the pores are filled with $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ (in which KF is not soluble). By using simple differences in solubility, only the surface of TO-MOF was deprotected (Fig. 11). The surface-deprotected TO-MOF was then treated with ethidium bromide monoazide, which upon coupling to the crystal surface was detected by fluorescence emission. Subsequent to surface modification, the interior of TO-MOF was deprotected using a TEAF in $\mathrm{THF/H_{2}O}$ solution. With the interior TMS groups now removed, the newly exposed alkynes were combined with benzyl azide using Click chemistry. Analysis of the framework postmodification by IR, gas sorption analysis, and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR confirmed the spatially confined modification.
The TMS-deprotection reactions described above represent a type of PSM and an alternative route for including reactive species into MOFs. Similar to transformation reactions, deprotection reactions can be used to effectively unmask specific functional groups that can then undergo subsequent modification. Yamada and Kitagawa described one example of a deprotection reaction on a MOF occurring in situ.38 In their study, 2,5-diacetoxyterephthalic acid was used to form a MOF with bipyridine and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . The resulting MOF was found to have the acetoxy groups removed yielding free hydroxyl groups in the framework. No specific mechanism was given for the unexpected deprotection, but the results foreshadowed the promise of using postsynthetic deprotection as a method for obtaining MOFs with useful functional groups.
Telfer and coworkers took the deprotection technique a step further and performed a more deliberate study using a thermally labile protecting group, Boc $\mathbf{Boc}\ =\ t e r t$ -butoxycarbonyl).39 The Boc protecting group is known to break down into $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and isobutylene when heated to high temperatures (Fig. 12). Telfer and coworkers used Boc protected 2-amino- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ - biphenyldicarboxylic acid to make a noncatenated analog of IRMOF-9 (Fig. 2).9 In order to remove the Boc protecting groups, the MOF was heated in DMF within the range of $150{-}200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Based on PXRD and single crystal X-ray analysis, the newly deprotected MOF was found to maintain crystallinity upon thermolysis. The MOF was analyzed by $\mathrm{{}^{1}H\ N M R}$ , IR, and TGA to verify that the deprotection reaction produced the amine-bearing MOF. Attempts were made to verify the expected pore volume increase after Boc deprotection through $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ gas sorption, but both the protected and deprotected MOFs underwent framework collapse upon solvent removal. Therefore, no gas sorption information on the effect of the removal of the BOC protecting groups was obtained.
## 2.3 Metals and coordinate covalent modification via PSM
It is often difficult to predict de novo whether or not a MOF will have unsaturated metal centers at the SBUs. However, there are several possible routes for obtaining a MOF with unsaturated or functional metal centers. One approach involves using an organic ligand that contains two or more types of metal binding sites. Under the right synthetic conditions, one of the metal binding groups may play a structural role (e.g., SBU formation) in the MOF while the other metal binding group is not involved structurally, and is available for introduction of additional metal ions. This strategy has been employed by Lin and coworkers to make a number of MOFs that can undergo coordinate covalent PSM with reagents such as $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ .23 Another example of this strategy utilized a MOF constructed from tetrakis(methylthio)-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (TMBD). This ligand contains two types of metal binding groups (i.e., carboxylates and thioethers) and forms a MOF termed PbTMBD when combined with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ .40 Single crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction confirmed that $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ was only bound at the carboxylate sites and not by the thioether groups. As a result, the thioether groups could be metallated in a PSM fashion by using $\mathrm{Hg}{\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$ in benzene. Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) analysis revealed that one $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ion was present per $\mathbf{Pb}^{2+}$ within PbTMBD. Single crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray data was collected for PbTMBD $\mathrm{Hg}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ to confirm the presence of the metal species. Suitable electron density corresponding to the $\mathrm{Hg}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ moiety was detected with reasonable $\mathrm{Hg-}\mathsf{S}$ distances of $2.685\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , but could not be conclusively assigned due to disorder. PXRD analysis of $\mathrm{PbTMBDcdotHgCl_{2}}$ provided compelling evidence for the presence of $\mathrm{Hg}{\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$ . Peak intensity differences in the PXRD spectra were observed after metallation, which were associated with changes in the framework porosity. Interestingly, the original PXRD pattern was restored after washing $\mathrm{PbTMBDcdotHgCl_{2}}$ with acetonitrile, which resulted in the removal of $\mathrm{Hg}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ . The metallation and removal process was repeated several times and was found to be reversible, which was confirmed by ICP and PXRD analysis.
In another interesting example, Yaghi and coworkers synthesized a $N$ -heterocyclic carbene (NHC) precursor ligand (4,7-bis(4-carboxylphenyl)-1,3-dimethylbenzimidazium tetrafluoroborate) and produced an IRMOF derivative denoted as IRMOF-76 (Fig. 13).41 Attempts to metallate IRMOF-76 in a coordinate covalent PSM manner by using $\mathrm{PdI}_{2}$ (pyridine) failed, despite using a variety of conditions. Ultimately, in order to obtain the desired ‘organometallic MOF’, the organometallated ligand ([4,7-bis(4-carboxylphenyl)-1,3-dimethylbenzimidazol-2-ylidene](pyridyl)palladium(II) iodide) was prepared and used in the direct solvothermal synthesis of IRMOF-77. Single crystal X-ray diffraction confirmed that $\mathrm{PdI}_{2}$ (pyridine) was attached to the ligand backbone. Accessibility to the bound $\mathrm{PdI}_{2}$ (pyridine) species was demonstrated by soaking IRMOF-77 in a solution of quinoline. After 24 hours, $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis indicated that the palladium centers had undergone ligand exchange, with pyridine completely displaced by quinoline, thus confirming that these sites were accessible and dynamic in IRMOF-77. This report shows an important example of direct synthesis prevailing over a PSM strategy, and highlights the challenges still associated with the use of PSM as a synthetic approach.
A second approach for obtaining unsaturated metal sites in MOFs is by combining covalent and coordinate covalent modification PSM. In this strategy, the MOF is appended with a chelating group and metallated afterwards. First reported by Rosseinsky and coworkers,24 this concept has been used by a number of other groups in order to produce MOFs that could potentially be used for gas sorption and catalysis applications. Hupp and Nguyen et al. obtained a $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ paddlewheel MOF with TCPB and DPG (meso-1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)-1,2-ethanediol) containing free diol groups, which they termed DO-MOF (Fig. 14). The diols were found to readily undergo modification with succinic anhydride resulting in a ring opened product with free carboxylic groups.42 Exposing the modified MOF to a solution of $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ indicated retention of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions, presumably due to coordination to the free carboxylate ligands.
Another covalent and coordinate covalent modification was performed by Yaghi and coworkers using UMCM- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ .43 The MOF was modified with 2-pyridinecarboxyaldehyde in toluene and metallated using $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (Fig. 15). UMCM- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was observed to undergo two distinct color changes: the light amber solid turned yellow upon imine formation and then purple upon $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ chelation. Elemental analysis indicated that quantitative modification and metallation had occurred and the BET surface area was found to drop significantly from $3200\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (unmodified $\mathrm{UMCM-l-NH}_{2}$ ) to $1700\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ (modified and metallated $\mathrm{UMCM-l-NH}_{2})$ . The metallated MOF was examined by Pd K-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) and X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectroscopy to confirm the presence of the bound $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ ion and to determine its coordination environment. By EXAFS, two Pd–Cl and $\mathrm{Pd-N}$ ligands with reasonable bond distances were detected. XANES provided further evidence that the $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ species present within the MOF was consistent with a $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ –Schiff base complex and not the metal source $\mathrm{(PdCl_{2}(C H_{3}C N)_{2})}$ ).
In a related example, Tanabe and Cohen modified UMCM- $\cdot\mathrm{l}{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with cyclic anhydrides that produced salicylate and pyrazine dicarboxylate chelating groups (Fig. 16).44 Reaction of UMCM-1– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ with 3-hydroxyphthalic anhydride or 2,3-pyrazinedicarboxylate resulted in UMCM-1–AMsal and UMCM-1–AMpz with percent conversions of $35\%$ and $50\%$ , respectively. Both modified MOFs were subsequently metallated with a variety of metal sources (e.g., ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2})$ . The metallated MOFs, UMCM-1–AMFesal and UMCM-1–AMCupz, were analyzed by atomic absorption (AA) analysis to quantify the metal content of the MOF. AA confirmed that UMCM-1–AMFesal contained $0.77~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ while UMCM-1–AMCupz had $1.76~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ These values roughly correspond to $\sim50\%$ metallation of the modified sites available within the framework. As further confirmation of the chelation, both metallated UMCMs were analyzed by diffuse reflectance solid state UV-Vis analysis. UMCM-1–AMFesal and UMCM-1–AMCupz showed distinct absorbance bands around $\sim400\mathrm{nm}$ and $\sim700\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, consistent with known $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ salicylate and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ pyrazine complexes.
## 3. Applications of MOFs modified via PSM
## 3.1 Improving and modulating gas sorption via PSM
An advantage of PSM is the ability to precisely tailor the pores of the MOF in order to improve host–guest interactions. The importance of MOFs as new $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sequestration materials makes the use of PSM to enhance these properties an attractive goal. For $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage, studies show that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding affinity in MOFs can be improved by having unsaturated metal centers present. One well-established method for achieving unsaturated metal centers in MOFs is by metal nanoparticle impregnation.45,46 MOFs have also been impregnated (doped) with metal ion clusters in order to bind $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ more strongly within the pores of the framework.47 Both techniques can be considered a form of PSM, but neither can be classified as covalent or coordinate covalent modification and hence will not be discussed here further.
An alternative method for incorporating unsaturated metal ions into MOFs is through ion exchange, which can be considered a form of coordinate covalent PSM. Modification by ion exchange is possible if the framework is anionic48 or the organic linkers have pendant groups that can be deprotonated and subsequently metallated. Hupp and coworkers used the ion exchange strategy to modify DO-MOF (Fig. 14) in order to improve $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake (Fig. 17).49 Coordinate covalent modification was used to convert the diol groups into lithium and magnesium alkoxides using either LiOtBu in $\mathrm{CH_{3}C N/T H F}$ or $\mathbf{Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ in MeOH. Different cation loadings were explored in order to obtain maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake. Higher loadings of either cation $(2.62\mathrm{\Li}^{+}$ to $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $2.02~\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ to $Z\mathrm n^{2+}$ ) resulted in not only a significant decrease in crystallinity and surface area, but also exhibited lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake than the unmodified DO-MOF. In order to preserve the framework, lower loadings of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ $\mathrm{[0.2\Li^{+}}$ to $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ $(0.86\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ to $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) were used. DO-MOF $\operatorname{Li}_{0.2}$ had a slightly higher $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ weight percent uptake over DO-MOF with an average of two additional $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ present. In contrast, DO-MOF $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mg}_{0.86}}$ did not show any improvement in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake. Interestingly, both DO-MOF $\operatorname{Li}_{0.2}$ and DO-MOF $\mathrm{Mg}_{0.86}$ showed heats of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ that increased with higher loading, which is not commonly seen in MOFs.
In contrast to the aforementioned metal-based strategies, Cohen and coworkers have explored the effect of organic functional groups on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake by using covalent PSM (Fig. 18).50 Three MOFs were tested for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption: IRMOF-3, DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , and UMCM-1– $\boldsymbol{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . IRMOF-3 was modified with three different functional groups using benzoic anhydride, phenyl isocyanate, and hexanoic anhydride to yield IRMOF-3–AMPh, IRMOF-3–URPh, and IRMOF-3–AM5, respectively. DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and UMCM- $\boldsymbol{1{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ were only modified with benzoic anhydride for comparison to IRMOF-3–AMPh. All unmodified and modified MOFs were tested for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption at $77\mathrm{K}$ and $87\mathrm{K}$ at 1 atm. The results indicated that both IRMOF-3–AMPh and –URPh showed higher weight percent uptake than IRMOF-3, especially at low pressure. In contrast, IRMOF-3–AM5 had lower weight percent uptake of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ when compared to IRMOF-3. Closer examination of the modified MOFs based on volumetric and a per molar basis showed that the phenyl modified MOFs clearly had an improved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity (Fig. 18). IRMOF-3–AMPh had an average increase of $1{-}2\ \mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per linker versus unmodified IRMOF-3, which is likely due to the added aromatic groups that produce additional binding sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ within the framework. The same trend was also observed with UMCM-1–AMPh, which had slightly higher uptake than UMCM- $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1-NH}_{2}}$ , but not as significant as the IRMOF-3 system. Although $\mathrm{UMCM-l-NH}_{2}$ is much more porous than IRMOF-3 and can uptake more guest molecules, the framework interactions with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are much weaker because of its large pore size.50 In contrast to both IRMOF-3–AMPh and UMCM-1–AMPh, DMOF-1–AMPh showed lower uptake over unmodified DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , which was attributed to the smaller pore size and loss of overall surface area after modification.
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sequestration and separation has emerged as a prominent goal in the MOF field. Preliminary data suggest that MOFs with polar or basic substituents can improve $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ interactions and uptake. Introduction of polar or basic substituents can be achieved by modifying the metal node or the organic ligand. One example of modifying the metal node for improving $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake was demonstrated by Hupp and coworkers. The MOF of interest was prepared from $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and TCPB (Fig. 19), which formed a 3D non-catenated paddlewheel MOF with the axial positions of the SBU coordinated by solvent (e.g., DMF).51 The MOF was prepared by three different ways: the as-synthesized MOF with DMF coordinated to the SBUs, the MOF with unsaturated metal sites upon DMF removal, and the MOF treated with trifluoromethyl pyridine $(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{-py)}$ coordinated to the unsaturated metal sites (produced by coordinate covalent PSM by exposing the MOF to $\mathrm{CF}_{3}–\mathfrak{p y}$ in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ ). Examination of all three MOFs revealed that the MOF with unsaturated metal sites, which also had the largest surface area, had the highest gas uptake with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , while $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ –py modified MOF had the lowest uptake. Based on the isotherms obtained for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) calculations were used to determine $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity for each of the MOFs. IAST calculations revealed that the $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ –py modified MOF displayed high selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures under low pressure. Despite its lower overall capacity, the combination of the $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ –py substituent along with constricted pore size was proposed as the source of the stronger $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ interactions within the MOF, thereby enhancing its selectivity over the other gases.
In another attempt to use coordinate covalent PSM to improve $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, Long and coworkers immobilized ethylenediamine onto unsaturated metal sites in a cubic sodalitetype MOF (Fig. 20). This MOF was constructed from ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and 1,3,5-tris( $1H.$ -1,2,3-triazol-5-yl)benzene $\mathrm{(H}_{3}[\mathrm{(Cu_{4}C l)}_{3}\mathrm{(BTTri)}_{8}])$ and both the parent $\mathbf{MOF}^{52}$ and ethylenediamine-modified MOF were examined for ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ .22 ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake was extremely low for both the parent MOF and modified MOF, but both MOFs showed significant uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . At extremely low pressures (up to 0.06 bar), the modified MOF showed a higher uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over the parent MOF. Unfortunately, the overall $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity for the modified MOF was disappointing at pressures beyond 0.1 bar, which was explained by crowding within the pores from the ethylenediamine moieties introduced via PSM. The modified MOF did show better selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\Nu_{2}$ than the parent MOF. A large difference between the parent and modified MOFs was seen after calculating the heat of adsorption at zero coverage. At zero coverage, the modified MOF had a record heat of adsorption $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ of $90\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , which was indicative of chemisorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to the ethylenediamine substituents (carbamate formation), while the parent MOF had a significantly lower $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}$ of ${\sim}20\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ .
In addition to investigations on gas sorption in MOFs for gas storage and capture applications, there has been substantial interest in understanding how MOF dynamics can influence gas sorption. Fe´ rey and Serre have extensively studied the gas sorption properties of MILs and other flexible
MOFs, which exhibit unique ‘breathing’ properties that are dependent on solvent, temperature, pressure, and the nature of the guest molecules.53 Breathing MOFs have flexible frameworks that undergo phase transitions (commonly between a narrow pore (np) and a large pore (lp) form), which can be monitored by gas sorption and PXRD. One clear distinction of breathing MOFs is that the topology, as well as the choice of building blocks, determines if the MOF will breathe or not. Interestingly, PSM has been shown to influence the breathing behavior of some MOFs. In a report by Wang and Cohen, it was demonstrated that the breathing properties of DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ could be altered upon modification with alkyl anhydrides (Fig. 21).54 DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ is a rigid framework that has Type 1 isotherm and does not show any breathing properties with ${\bf N}_{2}$ , Ar, or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption. DMOF- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was subjected to covalent PSM with alkyl chains of varying lengths (–AM1 to –AM6) and the gas sorption of the modified MOFs was analyzed by $\Nu_{2}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ . In principle, modified MOFs are expected to have lower surface areas when compared to the parent MOF due to the increased number of atoms introduced into the framework. As expected, DMOF-1–AM4, –AM5, and –AM6 showed much lower surface areas $(\sim700\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ than the parent MOF $(\sim1400\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and all showed typical Type I isotherms. Surprisingly, DMOF-1–AM1, –AM2, and –AM3 deviated from the expected trend. Both DMOF-1–AM1 and –AM2 had extremely low surface areas $(\sim300\mathrm{~\m}^{2}\mathrm{~\bf~g}^{-1})$ while DMOF-1–AM3 displayed unusual $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption resulting in low uptake of ${\bf N}_{2}$ at low pressure and a sudden increase in uptake of $\Nu_{2}$ at higher pressure (Fig. 21). Gas sorption of DMOF-1–AM3 with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $196\mathrm{K}$ displayed pronounced steps in the adsorption and desorption curves. A large increase in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was observed at $P/P_{\mathrm{o}}~=~0.12$ during adsorption with a large hysteresis present during desorption. Based on the distinct steps in the gas sorption isotherms, it was determined that DMOF-1–AM3 exhibited breathing behavior. The steps observed in the isotherms were attributed to structural transitions between a proposed np and lp form of the framework. Closer examination of DMOF-1–AM1 and –AM2 revealed that both MOFs displayed breathing, but were in the np forms, which explained why they both had extremely low surface areas. DMOF-1–AM3 was determined to be in an intermediate np–lp form while DMOF-1–AM4, –AM5, and –AM6 were all in the lp form.
## 3.2 MOF catalysts via PSM
PSM is an effective method for the design and discovery of new MOF catalysts. Through covalent and coordinate covalent PSM, a wide range of functional groups can be tethered to the metal nodes and organic ligand to transform MOFs into active catalytic materials. MOFs may exhibit unusual catalytic properties due to the constrained environment provided by the pores of the lattice (e.g., enantioselectivity, size selectivity). By using PSM, a single MOF lattice can be modified with various combinations of functional groups and thereby tested as a catalyst for different reactions. Moreover, once a potential MOF catalyst is identified, PSM can be used to systematically tune the functionality of the MOF in order to improve catalytic activity and selectivity.
By modifying the amine groups of IRMOF-3 with salicylaldehyde, followed by metallation with $\mathrm{NaAuCl}_{4}$ a catalytic MOF was obtained (IRMOF-3–SI–Au).55 Elemental analysis indicated that only $3\%$ of the amine sites were modified with the salicylaldehyde, but for those sites the metallation was quantitative. Despite having a low yield and hence small number of available active sites, IRMOF-3–SI–Au was found to be an active, single-site heterogeneous catalyst for domino coupling and cyclization of N-protected ethylaniline, aldehyde, and amines. IRMOF-3–SI–Au exhibited high activity when compared with control compounds (e.g., $\mathrm{\bfAu/ZrO}$ $\mathrm{Au(III)}$ Schiff base complex, and ${\mathrm{\bfAuCl}}_{3})$ ) with yields $>70\%$ in less than 24 hours. Moreover, the MOF was recyclable with up to four consecutive runs and remained stable, as confirmed by PXRD analysis of the MOF. In addition to domino coupling and cyclization, IRMOF-3–SI–Au also tested as an active catalyst for the hydrogenation of 1,3-butadiene. Recently, IRMOF-3–SI–Au was reported to be an active catalyst for cyclopropanation of alkenes with diazo compounds with modest product yields ranging from $25\mathrm{-}60\%$ in less than 24 hours.56
Another catalyst system was prepared via covalent and coordinate covalent PSM by using UMCM- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ . As mentioned earlier, $\mathrm{UMCM-l-NH}_{2}$ could be modified with cyclic anhydrides and metallated to obtain UMCM-1–AMFesal and UMCM-1–AMCupz (Fig. 15).44 UMCM-1–AMFesal was found to be an active, robust catalyst for the Mukaiyama aldol reaction, which involves C–C bond formation between aldehydes and silyl enols. The unmodified material, UMCM- $\cdot1\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , did not show any catalytic activity. UMCM-1–AMFesal catalyzed the reaction between 1-naphthaldehyde and 1-methoxy-2-methyl-1-(trimethylsiloxy)- propene up to $58\%$ with extremely low catalyst loading $(0.0001\ \mathrm{\mol\%}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ). Similar results were obtained using 1,3,5-mesitylaldehyde as well. UMCM-1–AMFesal was unable to catalyze the combination of either aldehyde with (1-tertbutylvinyloxy)trimethylsilane, which suggested that the catalyst was size selective based on the restricted size of the MOF pores.
Encouraged by the results obtained with UMCM-1–AMFesal, other metallated UMCM derivatives were prepared and explored for their ability to promote other Lewis acid catalyzed reactions.57 It was found that $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ could cleanly metallate the MOF, producing UMCM-1–AMInpz and UMCM-1–AMInsal with $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ loadings of $3.76~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(0.5\mathrm{mol\%})$ ) and $2.92~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(0.4\mathrm{mol\%}^{\cdot}$ ), respectively. Both catalysts, along with UMCM-1–AMFesal and UMCM-1–AMCupz, were examined for accelerating epoxide ring openings for $\upbeta$ -azido and $\upbeta$ -amino alcohol synthesis. Initial tests indicated that UMCM-1–AMInpz and UMCM-1–AMInsal were highly active for epoxide ring opening reactions, but only with specific nucleophiles. UMCM-1–AMInpz was more active with $\mathrm{TMSN}_{3}$ while UMCM-1–AMInsal was more active with aniline. Both MOFs performed as single-site catalysts that could be recycled up to three times with no signs of degradation or loss of activity. All of the metallated UMCM catalysts were further tested with different epoxides (e.g., cis-2,3-epoxybutane, cyclopentene oxide, styrene oxide, cis/trans-stilbene oxides). Interestingly, trans-stilbene was a poor substrate with either nucleophile and metallated MOF; however, the $\upbeta$ -azido alcohol product of cis-stilbene and $\mathrm{TMSN}_{3}$ was obtained using UMCM-1–AMInpz as the catalyst. The preference for cis over trans in these systems was confirmed by utilizing a $1:1$ mixture of cis/trans-stilbene oxide with $\mathrm{TMSN}_{3}$ and UMCM-1–AMInpz as catalysts. UMCM-1–AMInpz turned over cis-stilbene oxide selectively in this reaction mixture. These findings were in contrast to the same reaction catalyzed by a homogeneous catalyst (e.g., $\mathrm{Cr(salen)Cl}\ =\ (R,R)–N,N^{\prime}.$ -bis(3,5-di-tert-butylsalicylidene)-1,2-cyclohexanediaminochromium(III) chloride),58 which was found to turn over both cis and trans epoxides with no apparent selectivity. The apparent ‘MOF effect’ observed with UMCM-1–AMInpz as a selective catalyst for cis-stilbene oxide bodes well for the discovery of other MOF catalysts with unique properties derived from the framework structure.
In addition to metal-based catalysts, PSM is an excellent method to introduce organic functional groups into MOFs that impart hydrogen-bonding, acidic, and basic sites. This suggests that covalent PSM can be used to generate MOFs bearing organocatalytic groups. In one example, IRMOF-3 was partially modified with nicotinoyl chloride in pyridine and converted into an amide modified MOF with pendant pyridyl groups.59 Modification was confirmed by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis; however, traces of unreacted nicotinoyl chloride were detected within the pores of the MOF and the modified IRMOF-3 suffered a decrease in crystallinity due to the production of HCl as a byproduct of the PSM reaction. In the same study, another MOF, $Z_{\mathrm{nF(Am_{2}T A Z)}}$ (3,5-diamino-1,2,4-triazole), was also partially modified with pendant pyridyl groups and showed a better retention of crystallinity than the IRMOF-3 system. Both modified MOFs were tested as catalysts for the aza-Michael reaction between aliphatic amines and alkenes and for the transesterification of ethyldecanoate with MeOH. After 24 hours with $1.5\mathrm{mol\%}$ of either catalyst, the modified MOFs produced modest improvements $(5-40\%)$ in yield over unmodified IRMOF-3 and ${Z}_{\mathrm{nF(Am_{2}T A Z)}}$ .
Coordinate covalent PSM has also been used to introduce functional groups that impart new catalytic properties into a MOF. Kim and coworkers reported the first example of turning an achiral MOF into an active chiral catalyst by modifying unsaturated metal centers with a chiral L-proline derivative (Fig. 22).60 MIL-101(Cr), built from ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ trimer SBUs and BDC,61 contains unsaturated metal sites that are coordinated by solvent (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\it3}^{\prime}$ ) in the freshly prepared material. However, upon heat activation, the coordinated solvent was removed and subsequently replaced with L-proline derivatives, [(S)- $.N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -(pyridin-3-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide] or [(S)- $.N.$ -(pyridin-4-yl)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxamide], generating two modified MOFs (termed CMIL-1 and CMIL-2, respectively). CMIL-1 and CMIL-2 were examined as catalysts for asymmetric aldol reactions between aldehydes and ketones. The use of $10\ \mathrm{\mol\%}$ catalyst at ambient temperature resulted in yields from $60-90\%$ with ee’s ranging from
$55\text{\textperthousand}$ . As a comparison, the free L-proline ligands were tested and found to have much lower ee’s than when immobilized in the CMILs. Unmodified MIL-101(Cr) also showed insignificant activity $(<10\%)$ with no selectivity at all, thus confirming that the L-proline within the MOF were key to selective catalysis. The CMIL catalysts were recyclable up to three times; however, traces of the L-proline ligand were found to leach from the material after the fourth run.
Along with the reports of Lewis acid and organocatalysts, there has been interest in developing other types of MOF catalysts using PSM. One important area for exploration is oxidation catalysts.24 Fischer and coworkers described the covalent modification of the SBUs of MIL-53(Al) with ferrocene substituents and obtained a redox active MOF catalyst by PSM.62 MIL-53(Al) has infinite SBUs that are connected by bridging hydroxide groups. The bridging hydroxide groups were modified using $1,1^{\prime}$ -ferrocenediyl-dimethylsilane to incorporate the ferrocene group within the pores (Fig. 23). The modification was confirmed by cyclic voltammetry and $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ MAS NMR, which clearly showed distinct shifts from a ferrocene impregnated version of MIL-53(Al). Approximately $25\%$ of the SBUs in MIL-53(Al) were modified based on NMR and elemental analysis. The modified MIL-53(Al) was examined as a redox catalyst for benzene oxidation with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . A low, $15\%$ conversion of benzene to phenol was observed after 2 hours at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; however the unmodified MIL-53(Al) did not show any conversion of benzene to phenol, providing evidence that the modified system was an active catalyst due to the covalently attached ferrocene groups.
## 3.3 Manipulating and monitoring reactions via PSM
As described above, PSM has been used in developing functionalized MOFs and for applications such as improving gas sorption and developing new catalysts. However, PSM is not only a method of functionalizing MOFs, but it has also proven to be a valuable tool for understanding how reactions take place within a confined environment. Organic reactions can produce different outcomes when performed under homogeneous or heterogeneous conditions. In this respect, reactions can be studied using a MOF as a reaction vessel in order to obtain insight into reaction mechanisms and potentially control reaction outcomes.
Jones and Bauer took advantage of a MOF to explore the stereochemical outcomes of a bromination reaction with olefins.63 An interpenetrated MOF that was a structural analog of IRMOF-9 was prepared from $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and trans- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbene dicarboxylate (SDC). This lattice was then brominated at the central olefin of the ligand (Fig. 24). In principle, bromination of the olefin can result in either meso or enantiomeric products. The product outcome depends on the carbocation intermediate. Once the intermediate is formed, another bromide ion can attack and release ring strain to form the meso product. Alternatively, the $\mathrm{C-}C$ bond in the carbocation intermediate can rotate, thus relieving the ring strain before the attack of the bromide ion. Bromination of the free SDC ligand resulted in a $4:1$ mixture of meso : racemic product, as confirmed by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis. In contrast, bromination of the MOF at RT resulted in only the formation of the meso product in $60\%$ yield. It was proposed that immobilization of the stilbene unit within the MOF prevents free rotation around the $\mathrm{C-}C$ bond after the initial intermediate is formed, thus inhibiting the formation of the enantiomeric products.
In a particularly elegant experiment, PSM of a MOF has been used to capture reaction intermediates using single crystal X-ray diffraction.64 Fujita and coworkers demonstrated that aminotriphenylene ‘cartridges’ in a $Z\mathrm{nI}_{2}$ and 2,4,6-tris(4-pyridyl)- 1,3,5-triazine MOF can undergo PSM with anhydrides and aldehydes (Fig. 25). This MOF is particularly useful as it is one of the very few MOFs in which substituents introduced by covalent PSM can generally be located and assigned using single X-ray crystallography.65 Recognizing the advantage of this system, Fujita and coworkers used the porous, crystalline framework to monitor and trap kinetic intermediates. The reaction between the aminotriphenylene and acetaldehyde was examined in order to capture the hemiacetal intermediate in situ. Single crystal $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction, along with careful control of temperature and time, was used to collect data at various stages during the PSM reaction. First, an $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray structure of the MOF was taken prior to modification, which showed the unmodified amine groups. The crystal was then cooled down to $215\mathrm{K}$ and exposed to a stream of acetaldehyde in ethyl acetate for several minutes. To capture the reaction intermediate, the crystal was further cooled to $90\mathrm{K}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ -ray diffraction data were collected. After data collection, the temperature was raised to $270~\mathrm{K}$ in order to let the reaction proceed, and then upon re-cooling the crystal to $90~\mathrm{K}$ a final data set was collected. Analysis of each data collection revealed varying stages of modification. The unstable hemiacetal intermediate was captured at $215\mathrm{~K~}$ while the imine product was identified at $270\mathrm{K}$ . As an additional confirmation, singlecrystal microscopic infrared spectroscopy was performed at the same temperatures and distinct stretches were observed corresponding to the $-\mathrm{NH}$ stretch of the amine groups, the $-\mathrm{OH}$ stretch of the hemiacetal, and the $\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ stretch of the imine.
## 3.4 MOFs in biomedicine via PSM
The use of MOFs in biomedical applications is a relatively new field that has made significant progress in the last few years. The high porosities of MOFs result in exceptional guest uptake making them excellent as drug delivery vehicles. However, MOFs do face several challenges in drug delivery, including instability in biological media (i.e., $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and control over the rates of drug release. With respect to the latter, it is difficult to predict (and hence control) rates of drug release using MOFs because features such as pore size, shape, and the strength of host–guest interactions can all influence the release process.66 PSM can be used to improve drug delivery and release within MOFs by ‘loading’ the desired drug covalently onto the MOF. In this scenario, the drug can be effectively loaded and released upon decomposition of the MOF.
Lin and coworkers have utilized covalent PSM to attach drugs and imaging contrast agents to a MOF and tested these materials in cell culture.67 Nanoscale MOFs (NMOFs) of MIL-101(Fe) were prepared via microwave synthesis using $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and a mixture of $\mathrm{BDC}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ –BDC (Fig. 26). Two types of functionalized MIL-101(Fe) NMOFs were prepared via modification of the amine groups. In the first system, MIL-101(Fe) NMOFs were exposed to a solution of 1,3,5,7-tetramethyl-4,4-difluoro-8-bromomethyl-4-bora- $3a,4a\cdot$ diaza-s-indacene (Br–BODIPY) in THF to yield a BODIPY modified MIL. Between $20\text{\textperthousand}$ of the MOF linkers were modified with the dye, which was confirmed by UV-Vis spectroscopy based on the characteristic BODIPY absorption at $498~\mathrm{nm}$ . In the second modification, ethoxysuccinato-cisplatin $\mathrm{(ESCP~=~}c,c,t\mathrm{-[PtCl_{2}(N H_{3})_{2}(O E t)(O_{2}C C H_{2}C H_{2}C O_{2}H)]}$ ) was attached to the MIL-101(Fe) NMOFs by converting the carboxylic acid ligands of the $\mathrm{Pt}^{4+}$ compound into an activated ester and coupling it to the free amine groups of the MIL. Based on ICP-MS analysis, $\sim40\%$ of the amine sites were modified with ESCP. PXRD patterns showed that the crystallinity of the MILs was preserved and SEM images indicated that the crystal morphology was unchanged after either BODIPY or ESCP modification.
After successfully loading the MILs with BODIPY or ESCP, the MILs were immersed in buffer to determine the stability of the frameworks. Both MILs rapidly decomposed and released the modified ligands with a half-life of less than three hours. To improve the biological stability of the modified MILs, the crystals were coated with silica and reexamined. A significant improvement in stability was observed with the half-life increasing to $\sim16$ hours. The modified MILs were then examined in vitro with cancer cell lines. Laser scanning confocal microscopy confirmed that the BODIPY-modified MIL could cross the cell membrane and release the BODIPY dye inside of the cell. As a control experiment, the modified BODIPY ligand was incubated with the cells, but no BODIPY was detected at all within the cells, indicating that the MIL structure was required for cell uptake. The ESCP-modified MIL also successfully delivered cisplatin (ESCP is a cisplatin prodrug) to the cancer cell lines and was determined to have comparable cytotoxicity $(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\approx29\upmu\mathrm{M})$ when compared with cisplatin alone $(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\approx20~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ .
In another biomedical application of MOFs, these materials have been explored for nitric oxide (NO) delivery and release.
This topic was pioneered by Morris and co-workers, who described the direct coordination of NO to vacant metal sites in MOFs.68 However, NO can also react with amines to form $N$ -diazenium diolates (NONOates) that are easily released upon exposure to moisture. Using a coordinate covalent modification approach to introduce amine sites, Rosseinsky and coworkers described NO delivery and release from a MOF. HKUST-1 was targeted as a NO delivery system after undergoing coordinate covalent modification to introduce a secondary amine.69 HKUST-1 is built from 1,3,5-tribenzoic acid (BTC) and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ paddlewheel SBUs. 4-(Methylamino)- pyridine (4-map) was exposed to HKUST-1 via vapor diffusion at $413\mathrm{~K~}$ to allow 4-map to coordinate to the axial positions of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ paddlewheels (Fig. 27). The presence of 4-map was confirmed by the distinct color change of HKUST-1 (blue to green) and by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analysis of the digested MOF, which showed the presence of both BTC and 4-map. HKUST-1 was pretreated to remove any physisorbed 4-map and residual $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and was exposed to NO for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ . NO modification was found to be dependent on the percent conversion of HKUST-1 with 4-map. As modification increased (e.g., $97\%$ ), NO was unable to access the pores due to the bulky 4-map moieties. Low modification with 4-map (e.g., $32\%$ ) did not result in clean formation of the desired NONOates upon exposure to NO. Stable NONOate moieties were only obtained by tuning the HKUST-1 modification to $\sim60\%$ . Elemental analysis and NONOate stretches in the IR spectrum confirmed that at $\sim60\%$ loading of 4-map, subsequent exposure to NO resulted in the desired NONOate. NO was completely released when HKUST-1 was exposed to both air and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . One possible limitation of this system is that 4-map leaches from the HKUST-1 framework over time, suggesting that the coordinate covalent PSM is not entirely stable in this MOF.
Direct modification of MOFs with amine-bearing linkers (e.g., IRMOF-3) are also excellent candidates for NO delivery and release that would not be susceptible to the aforementioned leaching issue described above.70 With this objective in mind, IRMOF-3 and UMCM- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ were exposed to NO at room temperature for 24 hours to yield IRMOF-3–NONO and UMCM-1–NONO (Fig. 28). ATR-FTIR analysis of the modified MOFs showed new N–O $(1040-1043~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1310{-}1320~\mathrm{~cm}^{-1},$ and $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}N}$ $(1493-1505~\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ and $2270{-}2275~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ) stretches corresponding to the NONOates. Both IRMOF-3–NONO and UMCM-1–NONO also remained crystalline by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray analysis and maintained their microporosity ( $1294~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $2560~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively) as confirmed by BET surface area measurements with ${\bf N}_{2}$ .
To determine the amount of NO present in IRMOF-3–NONO and UMCM-1–NONO, the MOFs were digested and analyzed using a Griess assay, a commonly used assay to determine nitrite concentration. IRMOF-3–NONO was found to contain $0.51\pm0.11\mathrm{{\m}}$ mol of NO per gram of material while
UMCM-1–NONO contained $0.10~\pm~0.01$ mmol NO per gram of material. These values correspond to roughly $8\%$ and $6\%$ modification of amine sites within IRMOF-3 and UMCM- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , respectively. Despite the low modifications, the non-amine versions of IRMOF-3 and UMCM- $\mathsf{I}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (IRMOF-1 and UMCM-1) were exposed to NO under identical conditions and did not show any NO uptake.
## 3.5 Modulating the physical properties of MOFs via PSM
Cohen and coworkers proposed in an early PSM report that the physical properties of a MOF (i.e. porosity, hydrophobicity, chemical stability, etc.) could be manipulated by PSM.17 Modification of IRMOF-3 with alkyl anhydrides $\left(\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{n}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)_{2}$ where $n\ =\ 1$ to 18) to produce a series of isostructural modified MOFs bearing alkyl groups gave the first indication that modulation of physical properties was possible. These alkyl-modified MOFs showed different surface areas, based on the size of the alkyl substituent and extent of modification. Even more interesting was that IRMOF-3–AM19, which contained the longest alkyl chain, but a low degree of modification $(\sim7\%$ of the linkers were modified), was found to exhibit hydrophobic properties (e.g., immiscibility with water). This initial observation implied that certain types of functional groups could affect the physical properties of a MOF and perhaps even be used to stabilize the framework against moisture.71,72
To probe this observation in more detail, Nguyen and Cohen systematically examined the hydrophobicity and water stability of alkyl-modified IRMOF-3 materials using contact angle measurements, PXRD, and SEM analysis.73 A series of alkyl-modified IRMOF-3 materials (–AM1 through –AM6, –AM15, –AMiPr, –AMiBu) were prepared and compared against IRMOF-1 and unmodified IRMOF-3. IRMOF-1, IRMOF-3, and IRMOF-3–AM1 to –AM3 all had contact angles of ${\sim}0^{\circ}$ (i.e., were hydrophilic and absorbed water), while IRMOF-3–AM4, –AM5, –AM6, –AM15, –AMiPr, and –AMiBu all displayed contact angles $>\ 100^{\circ}$ , indicating hydrophobicity (Fig. 29). PXRD and SEM were used to observe how the new hydrophobic properties affected the modified IRMOF-3 samples. Exposure to ambient air over a period of four days showed by PXRD that IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-3 degraded. In contrast, the bulk crystallinity of IRMOF-3–AM6 and IRMOF-3–AM15 remained intact under the same conditions. Furthermore, upon direct exposure to liquid $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ for several minutes followed by examination by SEM, IRMOF-1 and IRMOF-3 displayed significant degradation and pitting of the MOF crystal surface, while IRMOF-3–AM6 and IRMOF-3–AM15 remained pristine and showed no changes due to contact with water.
In the same study, alkyl-modified versions of MIL-53(Al)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ were also examined by using contact angle measurements to see if these materials could also exhibit hydrophobic properties. MIL-53(Al)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ and MIL-53(Al)–AM1 were hydrophilic based on contact angle measurements, but MIL-53(Al)–AM4 and MIL53(Al)–AM6 exhibited superhydrophobic properties with contact angles $>150^{\circ}$ . It was proposed that the combination of the hydrophobic groups and smaller (microcrystalline) particle size of the MILs contributes to the observed superhydrophobicity.
## 4. Concluding remarks
PSM is an important approach for functionalizing MOFs with a wide range of groups that can enhance the chemical and physical properties of the framework. Through covalent and coordinate covalent PSM, various combinations of metal ions and organic components can be added to create frameworks of great complexity. PSM is a highly versatile method and allows for control over the number and types of functional groups introduced into a single framework, which is difficult to achieve through a direct synthetic route. In just the last two years, the progress in the PSM field has expanded greatly from simple one-step modifications to designing and testing modified MOFs for applications in gas sorption, catalysis, and biomedicine. Moreover, PSM has shown to be more than just a functionalization strategy, but as a tool to monitor organic reactions in the solid state and to manipulate the physical properties of the MOF. Although many of the applied studies presented here are in preliminary stages, the results provide a clear indication of the opportunities presented by using PSM to functionalize these hybrid materials and improve upon their usefulness in both fundamental and applied studies.
